WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.360
Why does your screen constantly move, update, and, you know, scroll, even when absolutely nothing in your actual life or your actual work has changed?

00:10.900 --> 00:16.360
Yeah, it is, it's basically the defining optical illusion of the 21st century.

00:16.780 --> 00:20.380
Welcome to today's Deep Dive, and I am looking right at you.

00:20.560 --> 00:26.080
Yes, you, the listener who is currently juggling like a dozen apps, drowning in notifications,

00:26.080 --> 00:32.500
and just trying to stay profoundly informed without feeling like you're suffocating under the weight of an infinite feed.

00:33.160 --> 00:33.800
We see you.

00:34.540 --> 00:35.960
Oh, absolutely. We've all been there.

00:36.220 --> 00:38.300
The question I just asked isn't rhetorical at all.

00:38.580 --> 00:47.400
It's actually the core puzzle we are unraveling today by exploring this dense, truly paradigm-shifting 2026 essay by the independent researcher Flyxion.

00:48.040 --> 00:49.740
Right, the never-ending ticker.

00:49.740 --> 00:56.400
Exactly. The never-ending ticker, quantification, attentional jurisdiction, and the right to finish.

00:57.020 --> 01:02.480
Okay, let's unpack this, because the premise here is wild, but it brings incredibly true.

01:03.280 --> 01:09.400
It argues that modern information systems have completely divorced your awareness from your competence.

01:10.100 --> 01:15.060
Yeah, they've built this society that is universally aware of every problem on the globe,

01:15.160 --> 01:19.260
but practically paralyzed when it comes to solving, well, a single one of them.

01:19.260 --> 01:21.620
And that viralysis isn't an accident, is it?

01:21.780 --> 01:23.200
No, it's by design.

01:23.460 --> 01:28.440
Or, I mean, at least it is the mathematical consequence of how our interfaces are designed.

01:28.880 --> 01:32.500
Flyxion actually starts with what they call the original observation.

01:33.080 --> 01:34.660
Right, the task manager thing.

01:35.040 --> 01:37.840
Yeah. So picture a piece of project management software.

01:38.500 --> 01:41.540
On the screen, you have these horizontal rows of task descriptions,

01:41.880 --> 01:45.760
and they are continuously drifting across your monitor in opposite directions.

01:46.300 --> 01:48.720
The rows literally never stop moving.

01:48.720 --> 01:50.480
It just keeps scrolling and scrolling.

01:50.900 --> 01:55.040
Exactly. But no tasks are being added, none are being completed.

01:55.620 --> 01:58.180
The underlying list of chores is totally static.

01:58.840 --> 02:03.480
So between one second and the next, the reality of your work hasn't changed one bit.

02:04.580 --> 02:09.060
Yet the screen is in this state of perpetual frantic motion.

02:09.060 --> 02:14.480
It's like the animated progress bar that just spins while the system is actually frozen.

02:14.980 --> 02:20.640
Or, you know, the news ticker at the bottom of a TV broadcast that just loops the same four headlines all day.

02:21.040 --> 02:27.020
The screen is screaming, like, things are happening, while the data is whispering, nothing has changed.

02:27.420 --> 02:28.260
That's a great way to put it.

02:28.340 --> 02:34.640
And Flyxion proved this mathematically with the representational motion theorem, or theorem 1.1.

02:34.640 --> 02:38.760
To understand it, we have to look at Claude Shannon's information theory.

02:38.960 --> 02:39.720
Oh, boy. Okay.

02:40.120 --> 02:41.040
Bear with me on the math.

02:41.180 --> 02:42.360
It's not too bad, I promise.

02:42.760 --> 02:50.760
In information theory, mutual information basically measures how much knowing one variable reduces your uncertainty about another.

02:50.760 --> 02:54.640
So novelty requires uncertainty to be resolved.

02:55.140 --> 03:03.000
So if I flip a trick coin that I know already has heads on both sides, the moment I look at the result, my information gain is exactly zero.

03:03.360 --> 03:04.900
Because I already knew what it was going to be.

03:05.260 --> 03:05.980
Precisely that.

03:06.540 --> 03:12.320
When you first open that task manager and glance at the static list, your uncertainty about your workload is resolved.

03:12.540 --> 03:13.420
You gain information.

03:13.680 --> 03:13.780
Right.

03:13.780 --> 03:23.040
But as the interface takes that exact same list and scrolls it predictably across the screen, it isn't giving you any new information about the state of your tasks.

03:23.620 --> 03:34.080
The theorem proves that while the information gain drops to zero and stays there permanently, the presentational activity, the literal distance the pixels have traveled on your screen, grows to infinity.

03:34.560 --> 03:34.880
Wow.

03:35.460 --> 03:40.340
So I am basically staring at a screensaver that is dressed up to look like an emergency.

03:40.740 --> 03:41.220
Exactly.

03:41.220 --> 03:47.300
To understand how we got here, the author breaks down the concept of the ticker into three evolutionary stages.

03:47.600 --> 03:48.340
Okay, let's go through those.

03:48.760 --> 03:50.120
The first is a physical device.

03:50.600 --> 03:52.820
Think of the 19th century stock ticker.

03:53.180 --> 04:00.000
It literally ticked, printing out numbers on a paper tape, but it only moved when a price actually changed on the exchange floor.

04:00.320 --> 04:02.940
So the motion was tethered to material reality.

04:03.540 --> 04:05.960
If the trading floor was quiet, the machine was silent.

04:06.500 --> 04:08.680
Yes, it was entirely event-driven.

04:08.680 --> 04:12.620
Then we moved to the second stage, which is the informational arrangement.

04:13.120 --> 04:15.260
This is the modern digital dashboard.

04:15.520 --> 04:18.120
Right, like the analytics pages on any software.

04:18.520 --> 04:26.700
Because the cost of displaying a metric dropped to basically zero, software engineers just started throwing every available number onto a screen.

04:26.700 --> 04:32.220
It doesn't matter if the person looking at the dashboard has the authority or the ability to change those numbers.

04:32.600 --> 04:34.660
It just looks comprehensive.

04:35.080 --> 04:40.160
Which leads us to the third stage, which I think is where the trap really snaps shut on you, the listener.

04:40.520 --> 04:41.740
The behavioral regulator.

04:41.740 --> 04:53.260
This is when the generalized social ticker, your feed, your constant notifications, your updating follower counts, makes you continuously aware of what can be measured, compared, and optimized.

04:53.640 --> 04:54.320
It's overwhelming.

04:55.080 --> 04:55.520
It is.

04:55.800 --> 04:59.220
The system manufactures an illusion of inexhaustibility.

04:59.220 --> 05:04.980
It implies urgency to capture your attention, completely untethered from the material state of the real world.

05:05.580 --> 05:09.080
So, representational motion replaces material progress.

05:09.680 --> 05:09.840
Okay.

05:10.120 --> 05:12.860
It's like sitting in a parked car in your driveway.

05:13.180 --> 05:18.920
But the dashboard is wired, so the speedometer is just endlessly racing between zero and 100.

05:19.300 --> 05:20.280
The engine is off.

05:20.680 --> 05:21.620
You're not going anywhere.

05:22.040 --> 05:25.580
But the interface is inducing this sheer panic of speed.

05:25.800 --> 05:27.600
That is the perfect analogy.

05:27.600 --> 05:30.920
But let me play the skeptic for a second here.

05:31.460 --> 05:35.860
If I know the Task app is just trying to look busy, why should I care?

05:36.600 --> 05:38.700
Isn't it just a harmless aesthetic choice?

05:39.020 --> 05:40.220
Like a design quirk?

05:40.700 --> 05:47.660
It stops being an aesthetic choice the moment those moving numbers are tied to your livelihood, your reputation, and your social standing.

05:48.300 --> 05:52.460
A moving display suggests incompletion without supplying evidence for it.

05:52.660 --> 05:53.440
What do you mean by that?

05:53.440 --> 05:57.980
Think about a static record, like a handwritten to-do list on a piece of paper.

05:58.400 --> 05:59.180
It just sits there.

05:59.560 --> 06:00.740
It allows you to audit it.

06:01.280 --> 06:03.400
Most importantly, it allows for closure.

06:03.800 --> 06:06.740
You cross the item off, and you see that it is done.

06:07.000 --> 06:07.260
Right.

06:07.340 --> 06:08.220
There's an end to it.

06:08.220 --> 06:08.860
Exactly.

06:09.480 --> 06:13.660
A moving ticker, by contrast, demands to be watched constantly.

06:14.580 --> 06:23.680
Systems financed by the harvesting of your attention have a deep underlying economic incentive to replace stable, finishable records with moving displays.

06:24.080 --> 06:26.620
Because if you can finish, you can leave.

06:26.620 --> 06:29.020
And they definitely can't monetize you leaving.

06:29.280 --> 06:29.760
Exactly.

06:30.240 --> 06:35.160
This bleeds perfectly into the next part of the deep dive, the quantification of existence.

06:35.600 --> 06:43.500
Once we understand that the ticker creates a false sense of perpetual motion, we have to look at what happens to human beings trapped inside this environment.

06:43.500 --> 06:48.020
Yes, the essay brings up Jaron Lanier's 2013 book, Who Owns the Future?

06:48.580 --> 06:51.040
Lanier was trying to solve a huge problem with the internet, right?

06:51.320 --> 06:53.680
He was trying to solve the invisible labor problem.

06:54.300 --> 06:56.760
Lanier pointed out this deep economic injustice.

06:57.740 --> 07:05.400
Giant tech companies extract immense value from the data, the photos, and the behavioral patterns of millions of people, but they don't pay us for it.

07:05.560 --> 07:06.440
We are the product.

07:06.540 --> 07:06.740
Right.

07:07.780 --> 07:11.820
Lanier's proposed alternative was a system where data provenance is tracked.

07:11.820 --> 07:18.800
You would get paid micro royalties every time your data or your posts were used to train an algorithm or target an ad.

07:19.440 --> 07:22.120
Which sounds incredibly fair on the surface.

07:22.500 --> 07:22.660
Yeah.

07:22.760 --> 07:24.820
If I generate the value, pay me the pennies, right?

07:25.380 --> 07:29.460
But Flyxion points out the devastating paradox buried in that utopia.

07:30.180 --> 07:35.520
To compensate everyone equitably for their data, you have to measure absolutely everything they do.

07:35.520 --> 07:42.820
And if you measure everything, you create a continuous running tally of human worth at every single moment of a person's life.

07:43.400 --> 07:48.620
So to fix the invisible labor problem, we accidentally mandate hyper-visibility.

07:48.920 --> 07:49.760
Everything is tracked.

07:50.340 --> 07:53.620
And visibility to oneself is not a neutral psychological state.

07:53.620 --> 08:00.180
The essay brings up a fascinating and honestly slightly terrifying historical case study, the clout score.

08:00.600 --> 08:02.200
Oh man, clout.

08:02.480 --> 08:15.080
For anyone who missed this era of the internet, around like 2011, you would link your Twitter, your Facebook, your LinkedIn, and this startup would spit out a number from 1 to 100, telling you exactly how influential you were.

08:15.360 --> 08:16.920
What happened when people got their scores?

08:16.920 --> 08:18.060
It was madness.

08:18.800 --> 08:20.800
People were putting their clout scores on their resumes.

08:21.540 --> 08:24.720
Airlines were giving lounge access to people with scores over 50.

08:25.340 --> 08:27.920
But the crazy part was how it changed how my friends acted.

08:28.340 --> 08:28.700
How so?

08:29.080 --> 08:33.920
They stopped posting normal updates and started trying to, you know, hack the algorithm.

08:34.660 --> 08:38.520
They would pick fights online just to get replies because replies boosted the score.

08:38.960 --> 08:42.420
Which is the perfect illustration of what Flyxion is analyzing here.

08:42.420 --> 08:47.920
Clout started as a measure of influence, but very quickly became a set of instructions for behavior.

08:48.320 --> 08:49.880
The measure swallowed the reality.

08:50.520 --> 08:52.300
People always bring up Goodhart's law here, right?

08:52.620 --> 08:55.540
When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.

08:55.820 --> 08:56.260
They do.

08:56.680 --> 09:01.800
But Flyxion makes a really vital distinction between Goodhart's law and Donald Campbell's law.

09:02.420 --> 09:06.600
They are often conflated, but they describe two totally different mechanisms of failure.

09:07.120 --> 09:08.160
Okay, let's break that down.

09:08.160 --> 09:13.540
Goodhart, who was an economist, was originally talking about statistical regressional overstatement.

09:13.840 --> 09:15.760
That is passive selection noise.

09:16.140 --> 09:16.400
Okay.

09:17.060 --> 09:19.200
Pretend I haven't taken statistics in 10 years.

09:19.420 --> 09:21.300
What is passive selection noise?

09:22.040 --> 09:22.400
Sure.

09:22.860 --> 09:29.000
Imagine you want to find the most dedicated employees, and you use hours logged at the desk as your proxy metric.

09:29.620 --> 09:32.220
Even if the employees don't know they are being measured,

09:32.580 --> 09:36.980
the group you select will naturally have their true dedication overstated by the proxy.

09:36.980 --> 09:37.880
Oh, I see.

09:38.160 --> 09:42.980
You'll accidentally sweep up the people who are just inefficient, or the people who fall asleep in their chairs.

09:43.340 --> 09:47.100
That is passive distortion through statistical regression to the mean.

09:47.480 --> 09:47.640
Got it.

09:47.860 --> 09:50.700
So the metric is just noisy and inaccurate.

09:51.340 --> 09:54.760
But Campbell's law is about active behavioral distortion.

09:55.560 --> 09:57.120
Metric-induced goal displacement.

09:57.740 --> 10:02.660
This is what happens when human agents know they are being measured, and that measure is attached to a reward.

10:03.120 --> 10:04.580
So they game the system.

10:04.580 --> 10:11.940
They actively change their behavior to optimize the proxy, even if it actively destroys the actual value they are supposed to be creating.

10:13.140 --> 10:19.680
Flyxion models this mathematically using a version of the multitask model developed by economists Holmstrom and Milgram.

10:19.680 --> 10:24.780
I am looking at this math in the notes, and honestly, it's brutal.

10:24.940 --> 10:28.940
Walk us through how this multitask model actually works in practice.

10:29.780 --> 10:30.760
Imagine a teacher.

10:31.240 --> 10:33.100
Their job consists of two tasks.

10:33.860 --> 10:37.140
Task one is teaching kids to pass a standardized math test.

10:37.140 --> 10:44.940
Task two is mentoring kids, helping them develop emotional resilience, and inspiring a genuine love of learning.

10:45.440 --> 10:47.240
Both of which have true value to society.

10:47.460 --> 10:47.880
Exactly.

10:48.800 --> 10:51.080
Now, the school district comes in and says,

10:51.740 --> 10:56.800
We are attaching a massive financial bonus to task one, the test scores.

10:57.180 --> 11:02.560
Because task one is easy to measure, and task two is basically impossible to quantify.

11:02.560 --> 11:11.820
The math models human effort as a substitute good, meaning there are only so many hours in a day, and only so much energy in a human body.

11:12.500 --> 11:17.080
Trying really hard at one thing mathematically restricts your ability to try hard at another.

11:17.420 --> 11:18.700
Right. That makes sense.

11:19.060 --> 11:24.280
As the reward for the measured task climbs, the teacher naturally shifts effort toward it.

11:24.600 --> 11:27.800
But they have to pull that effort away from the unmeasured task, too.

11:28.380 --> 11:29.320
They stop mentoring.

11:29.840 --> 11:31.240
They just drill test prep.

11:31.240 --> 11:35.520
Which is really sad, but maybe you think, well, at least the math scores are going up.

11:35.880 --> 11:37.460
And that's where the math bites you.

11:37.920 --> 11:43.120
The proof shows that at a certain threshold, as the incentive for the measured task continues to climb,

11:43.440 --> 11:49.800
the total gross output value, the combined true value of the student's education to society, actually drops.

11:50.060 --> 11:50.700
Right. Really?

11:50.960 --> 11:51.240
Yes.

11:51.540 --> 11:54.960
The incentive attached to the proxy metric doesn't just plateau.

11:55.300 --> 11:57.500
It actively damages the holistic work.

11:57.500 --> 12:01.780
A strong enough pressure on a partial metric replaces the core mission entirely.

12:01.780 --> 12:05.060
So the incentive destroys the overall value.

12:05.060 --> 12:10.400
And this pressure is compounded by how these metrics are calculated over time, isn't it?

12:11.040 --> 12:17.620
Because the essay highlights the mathematical difference between accumulating metrics and decaying metrics.

12:17.620 --> 12:21.040
This is a crucial hidden architecture in our digital lives.

12:21.600 --> 12:24.140
An accumulating metric is like a historical record.

12:24.300 --> 12:27.000
It's an integral function of your effort over time.

12:27.180 --> 12:28.000
Like getting a degree.

12:28.260 --> 12:28.560
Exactly.

12:28.780 --> 12:32.940
If you write a novel or you earn a college degree, that accomplishment is yours forever.

12:33.420 --> 12:34.340
You can stop writing.

12:34.520 --> 12:35.320
You can stop studying.

12:35.520 --> 12:37.500
And the fact that you did the work doesn't disappear.

12:37.760 --> 12:38.600
The sum is static.

12:38.600 --> 12:41.980
But a decaying metric is something entirely different.

12:42.660 --> 12:45.320
A decaying metric applies a geometric discount rate.

12:45.640 --> 12:51.840
Think of your credit score or an algorithm's estimation of your social media relevance or a gig economy driver's rating.

12:52.340 --> 12:54.460
If you stop participating, your score doesn't freeze.

12:54.760 --> 12:55.820
It actively drops.

12:56.180 --> 12:57.760
Oh, that's stressful just to think about.

12:58.180 --> 12:59.840
The math here is incredibly stark.

12:59.840 --> 13:10.040
A score with a geometric decay rate requires a perpetual, infinite stream of ongoing contributions just to hold the score steady at a positive level.

13:10.860 --> 13:12.760
I want you, the listener, to really picture this.

13:13.060 --> 13:14.980
It is exactly like a hamster wheel.

13:15.680 --> 13:17.040
The wheel isn't just a place to run.

13:17.620 --> 13:25.520
The wheel is designed so that if you just want to stand perfectly still, if you just want to maintain your current position in the cage, you have to actively sprint.

13:25.940 --> 13:26.800
That's exactly it.

13:26.800 --> 13:29.780
The moment you stop to breathe, the wheel throws you backward.

13:30.340 --> 13:32.760
Why on earth would engineers design systems this way?

13:33.180 --> 13:38.520
Well, there is actually a legitimate statistical reason to use decay in certain specific contexts.

13:39.100 --> 13:42.180
Flyxion brings up the Kalman filter to explain this.

13:42.400 --> 13:43.400
A Kalman filter?

13:44.080 --> 13:44.620
What is that?

13:45.000 --> 13:52.580
It's an algorithm used in engineering and statistics to estimate the true state of a system from a series of noisy measurements over time.

13:52.580 --> 14:01.000
Think of it like trying to guess the exact location of a car driving through a long tunnel, but you only get brief glimpses of it when it passes under the occasional streetlight.

14:01.500 --> 14:02.340
Okay, I can picture that.

14:02.480 --> 14:08.680
The algorithm uses exponential smoothing, which is a mathematical form of decay, to predict where the car is in the dark.

14:08.680 --> 14:13.840
So it weights the most recent streetlight glimpse much more heavily than the glimpse from five minutes ago.

14:14.220 --> 14:14.460
Yes.

14:15.140 --> 14:25.240
If you are trying to estimate someone's present competence at a highly degradable skill, you legitimately should weight their performance from yesterday more heavily than their performance from a decade ago.

14:25.820 --> 14:31.900
If I am getting on an airplane, I want the pilot's rating to reflect how they flew this month, not just how they flew in 2010.

14:32.140 --> 14:33.060
It makes total sense.

14:33.420 --> 14:35.280
Decay is a tool for accurate inference.

14:35.280 --> 14:37.580
But here is the pathology.

14:38.380 --> 14:49.560
When a decaying score is taken out of the context of inference and is instead used as an incentive that is displayed to the user and tied to their livelihood, it transforms into a disciplinary device.

14:49.780 --> 14:50.400
It becomes a weapon.

14:50.680 --> 14:50.940
Yes.

14:51.460 --> 14:53.360
It creates a perpetual obligation.

14:54.180 --> 14:56.560
The system is no longer just trying to estimate your skill.

14:57.080 --> 15:01.660
It is relying on your continued frantic activity to fuel its own data needs.

15:01.660 --> 15:06.660
And it borrows the authority of statistical inference to justify making you run on the hamster wheel.

15:07.380 --> 15:14.500
So we have individuals sprinting on metric hamster wheels, chasing decaying scores that force them to abandon unmeasured meaningful work.

15:15.140 --> 15:17.100
But what does this mean when we zoom out?

15:17.360 --> 15:25.240
Like, if this metric-induced distortion breaks the individual, what happens when this continuous stream of demands is scaled up to society as a whole?

15:25.240 --> 15:33.880
To understand the societal scale, Flyxion points us back to the economist Herbert Simon, who coined the concept of the attention economy way back in 1971.

15:34.300 --> 15:34.780
1971.

15:35.200 --> 15:35.360
Wow.

15:35.460 --> 15:37.840
He saw this coming a long time ago.

15:37.900 --> 15:38.420
He really did.

15:39.200 --> 15:41.820
Simon observed a fundamental asymmetry in human progress.

15:42.300 --> 15:48.600
The availability of information can grow exponentially because storage and transmission costs continually fall.

15:49.060 --> 15:53.960
But human processing capacity, our biology, our frontal lobes, is strictly bounded.

15:54.080 --> 15:54.840
It does not scale.

15:54.840 --> 15:56.700
We only have so much brain power.

15:57.060 --> 16:00.840
Therefore, a wealth of information necessarily creates a poverty of attention.

16:01.480 --> 16:02.680
I love bringing it back to biology.

16:03.780 --> 16:05.980
The problem isn't a lack of discipline on my part.

16:06.280 --> 16:11.380
The problem is that the environment has fundamentally diverged from my biological capacity to process it.

16:11.820 --> 16:19.280
To prove how catastrophic this is, the essay formalizes this using queuing theory, specifically the MM1 queuing model of information.

16:19.280 --> 16:25.480
I'm looking at this model, and it's usually used for factory assembly lines or computer network servers, right?

16:25.480 --> 16:28.080
How does it apply to human empathy and attention?

16:28.080 --> 16:33.740
Think of your attention as the server and the world's demands as the data packets.

16:33.740 --> 16:45.080
In the M-slash-M-slash-1 model, messages are arriving at a certain rate, which mathematicians call lambda, and you, the human, can process them at a certain rate called mu.

16:45.080 --> 16:46.080
Okay.

16:46.080 --> 16:48.080
Lambda is the incoming demands.

16:48.080 --> 16:50.440
Mu is my brain's processing speed.

16:50.440 --> 16:51.440
Exactly.

16:52.100 --> 17:00.560
The model assumes that relevant, actionable messages and completely irrelevant, non-actionable messages are mixed together, arriving indistinguishably.

17:00.960 --> 17:09.460
If the total volume of messages, lambda, stays safely below your processing capacity, mu, you can eventually sort through the queue and get to everything.

17:10.040 --> 17:12.000
But what happens when lambda gets close to mu?

17:12.420 --> 17:17.560
Which, let's be honest, is basically everyone's email inbox, social feed, and Slack channel right now.

17:17.560 --> 17:21.280
We are constantly operating at like 99% capacity.

17:22.000 --> 17:25.500
The math shows a catastrophic non-linear degradation.

17:26.020 --> 17:27.340
It's not a gentle slope.

17:27.680 --> 17:28.380
It's a cliff.

17:28.780 --> 17:30.840
Let's say actionable messages have a deadline.

17:31.440 --> 17:34.180
If you don't read them in time, the opportunity to help is lost.

17:34.900 --> 17:45.660
As the total volume of messages approaches your maximum processing capacity, as the ratio of lambda to mu approaches 1, the delay for any given message shoots toward infinity.

17:45.660 --> 17:48.100
The backlog compounds on itself.

17:48.540 --> 17:54.320
The probability of you finding and acting on a relevant message before its deadline plummets toward absolute zero.

17:54.840 --> 17:57.360
Because I'm spending all my time just triaging the noise.

17:57.880 --> 18:03.820
If I can't perfectly filter the actionable from the non-actionable at the exact millisecond they arrive,

18:03.820 --> 18:12.720
then every single irrelevant tragedy broadcast into my feed actively degrades my ability to respond to the important things happening right in front of me.

18:13.120 --> 18:13.340
Yes.

18:13.560 --> 18:18.000
And this leads to what the author calls responsibility without jurisdiction.

18:18.320 --> 18:20.660
It is the globalization of concern.

18:21.140 --> 18:21.560
Think about it.

18:21.580 --> 18:26.840
A century ago, your awareness of human suffering was largely limited to your geographic community.

18:26.840 --> 18:30.240
You knew about the barn that burned down down the road.

18:30.840 --> 18:32.780
And you could actually go help rebuild it.

18:32.900 --> 18:33.280
Exactly.

18:34.120 --> 18:40.020
Today, you can pull a piece of glass out of your pocket and within three minutes watch a famine unfold across the world,

18:40.440 --> 18:46.220
see a corporate scandal in a city you've never visited, and read about a legislative crisis on another continent.

18:46.840 --> 18:52.020
The presentation of these problems on your feed implies that you are supposed to do something about them.

18:52.420 --> 18:56.440
Or, at the very least, feel the appropriate amount of agonizing guilt.

18:56.440 --> 18:58.860
But I can't do anything about most of them.

18:59.380 --> 19:01.700
I'm just a person sitting on my couch in my living room.

19:01.800 --> 19:02.880
I don't command an army.

19:03.280 --> 19:04.900
I don't control the federal budget.

19:05.380 --> 19:11.140
To map out why this feels so terrible, the essay separates our relationship to problems into a matrix of four conditions.

19:11.800 --> 19:15.420
Knowledge, care, duty, and ability.

19:15.740 --> 19:16.980
Let's expand on this matrix.

19:17.340 --> 19:18.860
What does it look like in real life?

19:19.520 --> 19:20.880
Let's compare two people.

19:21.300 --> 19:25.860
First, look at a local mayor whose town budget has just been slashed by the state.

19:26.440 --> 19:29.080
That mayor has the knowledge of the potholes in the road.

19:29.640 --> 19:31.920
They have the care, because it's their town.

19:32.260 --> 19:35.240
They have the duty, because they were elected to fix it.

19:35.540 --> 19:39.900
But they lack the ability, the cause, the levers, the money to actually intervene.

19:40.400 --> 19:42.380
That is a painful state of impotence.

19:42.800 --> 19:47.780
Now, look at you, the private citizen watching a push notification about a distant famine.

19:47.780 --> 19:50.800
You have the knowledge, and you legitimately care.

19:51.420 --> 19:54.720
But you don't have an assigned duty, and you certainly lack the ability.

19:55.600 --> 20:02.080
The tragedy of modern information networks is that they aggressively manufacture the specific scenario at an industrial scale.

20:02.760 --> 20:07.020
Universal knowledge and care combined with absolutely zero duty or ability.

20:07.560 --> 20:12.400
We've built an infrastructure that systematically generates guilt without supplying causal levers.

20:12.400 --> 20:13.120
Exactly.

20:14.240 --> 20:16.120
I mean, it is devastatingly effective.

20:16.960 --> 20:23.620
And when humans are placed in a condition of intense, pressured concern, but lack the material means to intervene,

20:23.940 --> 20:27.920
they are forced to turn to the economy of symbolic participation.

20:28.540 --> 20:29.260
Meaning posting?

20:30.100 --> 20:30.560
Liking.

20:31.200 --> 20:32.240
Retweeting the crisis.

20:32.760 --> 20:34.720
Putting a filter on my profile picture.

20:34.980 --> 20:38.620
The essay models this using a linear programming model of moral effort.

20:38.620 --> 20:42.460
In linear programming, you have an objective function you want to maximize or minimize,

20:42.720 --> 20:45.820
subject to certain constraints like time, money, or social capital.

20:46.220 --> 20:48.140
So what's the objective function here?

20:48.760 --> 20:53.340
Here, your brain's objective function is to minimize the psychic discomfort of guilt,

20:53.460 --> 20:55.820
while maximizing the perceived moral action.

20:56.220 --> 21:03.560
When confronted with a crisis, you can choose material action, which is expensive, slow, hard to verify, and might completely fail.

21:03.560 --> 21:11.440
Or you can choose symbolic action, which is immediate, socially visible, incredibly cheap, and makes you feel like you did something.

21:11.960 --> 21:15.240
So symbolic action effectively hacks the constraint equation.

21:15.620 --> 21:15.940
Yes.

21:16.480 --> 21:20.540
The math proves that if the cost of symbolic action drops low enough,

21:20.660 --> 21:27.360
and if the human brain experiences symbolic action as roughly equivalent to material action and relieving that inner guilt pressure,

21:27.360 --> 21:34.740
then material effort drops to literally zero, because the brain rationally takes the path of least resistance to relieve the pressure.

21:35.140 --> 21:37.040
We aren't bad, apathetic people.

21:37.480 --> 21:41.300
We're just optimizing our moral cost function in a completely broken system.

21:41.740 --> 21:42.340
Precisely.

21:42.760 --> 21:45.980
The moral costs of this perpetual exposure are just immense.

21:46.740 --> 21:47.500
The author lists

21:47.500 --> 21:49.300
attentional exhaustion.

21:50.140 --> 21:53.980
Your literal neurological capacity for sustained concern is depleted.

21:54.800 --> 21:55.680
Misplaced confidence?

21:55.680 --> 22:01.880
You think you understand a conflict's geopolitical conflict because you read a thread of six posts by a stranger.

22:02.720 --> 22:03.380
Shallow judgment.

22:04.180 --> 22:07.940
But the worst one, to me, is the displacement of local obligations.

22:08.200 --> 22:09.400
It's a huge problem.

22:09.760 --> 22:14.660
The energy you spend emotionally agonizing over a distant crisis you cannot change

22:14.660 --> 22:19.160
is finite energy that you withdraw from your family, your workplace, and your local community.

22:19.300 --> 22:21.880
The exact problems you actually could have solved.

22:22.320 --> 22:25.440
Which brings us to a highly controversial defense of logging off.

22:25.440 --> 22:27.520
The author calls it rational disengagement.

22:28.240 --> 22:29.400
I have to ask about this.

22:29.560 --> 22:29.740
Yeah.

22:30.140 --> 22:33.560
Is muting notifications or ignoring the news a sign of apathy?

22:34.020 --> 22:38.660
Because, you know, society really makes you feel like a terrible citizen if you step away from the feed.

22:39.240 --> 22:40.700
Silence is violence, right?

22:41.280 --> 22:43.640
The author gives a definitive, rigorous no.

22:43.640 --> 22:46.440
Remember the MM1 cueing model.

22:46.440 --> 22:54.860
When you cannot filter the actionable from the non-actionable, muting the feed is a highly logical, rational inference about a noisy environment.

22:55.000 --> 22:56.160
It's just preserving bandwidth.

22:56.540 --> 22:59.600
It is a defense mechanism to preserve your capacity to act.

22:59.980 --> 23:03.080
It is not a moral verdict on the importance of the world's problems.

23:03.540 --> 23:06.840
It is a verdict on the usefulness of the broadcast mechanism.

23:07.300 --> 23:10.800
Muting is how you survive so you can actually do something useful offline.

23:10.800 --> 23:20.160
If broadcasting all problems to all people just causes paralysis, exhaustion, and symbolic posturing, how are we supposed to allocate our attention?

23:20.960 --> 23:21.620
How do we filter?

23:22.500 --> 23:24.560
And that brings us to the proposed cure.

23:25.200 --> 23:27.060
The Mind Your Own Business Protocol.

23:27.780 --> 23:28.980
Or MYOB.

23:28.980 --> 23:37.400
I love this protocol because it takes a phrase usually used to rudely dismiss people and elevates it into a rigorous ethical framework.

23:37.940 --> 23:45.020
The author proposes a radical redefinition of what local responsibility actually means, and it starts with the leaky pipe example.

23:45.980 --> 23:47.720
Okay, I love a good plumbing analogy.

23:48.320 --> 23:51.760
If water is dripping from my ceiling, that is a local problem.

23:52.200 --> 23:53.420
But what makes it local?

23:53.420 --> 23:56.240
It is not just geographic proximity.

23:57.120 --> 24:00.240
A problem is practically local to you when it meets four criteria.

24:00.980 --> 24:02.640
First, it has a discoverable location.

24:02.900 --> 24:03.860
You can see the water dripping.

24:04.600 --> 24:07.200
Second, it has an identifiable failure mechanism.

24:07.800 --> 24:11.220
You can cut a hole in the drywall, find the copper pipe, and see the crack.

24:11.460 --> 24:11.940
Makes sense.

24:12.780 --> 24:15.720
Third, you have an available repertoire of repairs.

24:16.080 --> 24:19.880
You can grab a wrench, drive to the hardware store, buy a new joint, and swap it out.

24:19.880 --> 24:23.660
And fourth, it has observable criteria for success.

24:24.300 --> 24:28.780
Meaning, I turn the water main back on, and I stand there and look at it to see if it drips.

24:29.600 --> 24:33.520
Observation, diagnosis, intervention, verification.

24:34.000 --> 24:35.880
Your proximity isn't just about distance.

24:36.000 --> 24:38.340
It's about your position of practical efficacy.

24:38.340 --> 24:50.680
A software developer fixing a bug in a server located 3,000 miles away has local access to that problem because they can see the code, diagnose the logic, push a patch, and run a test to verify it.

24:50.900 --> 24:51.160
Oh, wow.

24:51.380 --> 24:56.620
By contrast, a person watching a street brawl right outside their window might be geographically close.

24:57.040 --> 25:07.240
But if they don't know the parties, have no authority to intervene safely, and can't verify the aftermath, that problem is fundamentally less local to them than the bug is to the developer.

25:07.240 --> 25:09.040
That reframing is incredible.

25:10.060 --> 25:13.180
Locality is about agency, not just geography.

25:13.740 --> 25:16.920
And this leads to the four dimensions of the MYOB protocol.

25:17.720 --> 25:18.040
Yes.

25:18.300 --> 25:24.780
When deciding where to allocate your finite attention, you should prioritize problems where you possess four things.

25:25.440 --> 25:27.760
Observational access, you can see what's happening.

25:28.500 --> 25:30.960
Causal competence, you have the skills to intervene.

25:31.760 --> 25:35.580
Feedback reliability, you can tell if your intervention actually worked.

25:35.580 --> 25:36.960
And legitimacy.

25:37.880 --> 25:41.740
I want to dig into legitimacy because I feel like that's the one we forget the most on the Internet.

25:42.080 --> 25:43.300
It is the most important one.

25:44.340 --> 25:46.540
The essay formulates a readiness index.

25:47.060 --> 25:49.040
It's a multiplicative mathematical model.

25:49.560 --> 25:54.500
You multiply your access by your competence, by your feedback, by your legitimacy.

25:54.500 --> 26:02.500
Because it is multiplicative, if any single one of those factors is zero, your total readiness to intervene is mathematically zero.

26:02.500 --> 26:13.160
So even if I have 100% observational access, 100% competence, and 100% feedback reliability, if my legitimacy is zero, my right to intervene is zero.

26:13.800 --> 26:15.800
Competence is not entitlement.

26:16.160 --> 26:24.360
Just because you have the technical skill to hack into an institution's database and fix their inefficient filing system doesn't mean you have the legitimate authority to do it.

26:24.360 --> 26:31.180
Just because you think you know exactly how your neighbors should discipline their kids doesn't give you the standing to rearrange their household.

26:31.520 --> 26:32.160
It's boundaries.

26:32.760 --> 26:39.840
The protocol protects people from technocratic saviors who impose solutions without the consent of the affected parties.

26:39.840 --> 26:44.600
Let's talk about the math of how we actually spend our attention under this protocol.

26:44.940 --> 26:50.920
The essay brings up Proposition 9.1 and 9.2, the equi-marginal allocation of attention.

26:51.820 --> 26:55.360
What does this mean for you, the listener, trying to figure out what to care about today?

26:55.840 --> 26:59.780
It's a mathematical proof that utterly destroys how we currently operate.

27:00.440 --> 27:03.280
Currently, we allocate our attention based on visibility.

27:03.820 --> 27:08.280
How loud a problem is, how horrific the imagery is, how much it is trending on social media.

27:08.280 --> 27:19.880
The proof shows that allocating a finite budget of attention based on visibility is vastly inferior to allocating it based on the marginal expected corrective benefit.

27:20.440 --> 27:22.840
Marginal expected corrective benefit.

27:23.220 --> 27:25.460
That sounds like an economics textbook.

27:25.660 --> 27:26.440
Break that down for us.

27:26.640 --> 27:34.900
It means you should ask yourself, if I spend one more hour of my attention on this problem, how much actual real-world correction will occur?

27:34.900 --> 27:42.940
For a trending global geopolitical crisis, spending one more hour reading posts about it produces zero marginal corrective benefit.

27:43.480 --> 27:46.800
You learn nothing you can act on, and you change nothing.

27:47.460 --> 27:55.720
But spending one hour helping your kid with math, or reviewing a contract for your local community board, produces a massive marginal corrective benefit.

27:55.720 --> 27:56.440
Exactly.

27:57.140 --> 28:09.680
The math proves that maximizing human welfare requires us to shift our attention away from the highly visible problems we cannot solve, and aggressively toward the quiet local problems we can solve.

28:09.920 --> 28:11.240
But I have to push back here.

28:11.460 --> 28:12.400
I have to play devil's advocate.

28:13.460 --> 28:18.300
Am I supposed to just 10-mile and garden and completely ignore systemic injustice?

28:18.960 --> 28:20.080
Ignore climate change?

28:20.200 --> 28:21.740
Ignore distant emergencies?

28:21.740 --> 28:29.600
Isn't the MYOB protocol just a highly intellectualized, extremely convenient excuse for privilege and complacency?

28:29.980 --> 28:35.740
Like, oh, I can't personally fix global poverty, so I'm just going to focus on my golf swing and ignore the world.

28:36.460 --> 28:40.100
It is the most crucial objection, and Phloxion confronts it head-on.

28:40.660 --> 28:43.760
The MYOB protocol is a defeasible presumption.

28:44.100 --> 28:48.200
Meaning it's a default rule, but one that can be overridden by specific evidence.

28:48.200 --> 28:53.520
Exactly. It's a default stance of humility, not an absolute prohibition on caring.

28:54.180 --> 28:56.320
The text explicitly outlines three exceptions.

28:56.700 --> 28:57.580
First, emergencies.

28:58.160 --> 29:05.460
If a child is drowning in a shallow pond right in front of you, you don't calculate your jurisdiction or check your readiness index.

29:05.560 --> 29:06.300
You pull them out.

29:06.880 --> 29:10.140
This incorporates philosopher Peter Singer's famous rescue principle.

29:10.520 --> 29:11.660
Okay, that makes sense.

29:11.660 --> 29:14.020
Second, external harms.

29:14.640 --> 29:22.580
If your factory is polluting a river, you can't claim the downstream cancer cluster isn't your business just because it's outside your immediate visual horizon.

29:23.120 --> 29:26.460
You caused it, so your jurisdiction extends to it.

29:26.920 --> 29:29.420
What about big systemic issues like climate change?

29:29.820 --> 29:32.500
I didn't personally cause it in a vacuum, but it affects me.

29:32.720 --> 29:35.940
That falls under the third exception, institutional duties.

29:36.660 --> 29:38.660
Systemic problems require institutions.

29:38.660 --> 29:44.900
The protocol argues that your duty isn't to agonizingly monitor climate data on your phone and feel guilty.

29:45.540 --> 29:55.360
Your duty is to vote for, fund, and support the institutions and specialists who actually do have the observational access and causal competence to intervene at scale.

29:55.820 --> 29:57.540
Ah, so you delegate the jurisdiction.

29:57.980 --> 30:01.420
Furthermore, local includes institutional extensions.

30:01.420 --> 30:09.340
A climate scientist studying a distant glacier using satellite data and rigorous peer review does have local access, conceptually.

30:09.780 --> 30:12.200
They have the feedback mechanisms and the competence.

30:12.740 --> 30:14.540
So the protocol isn't saying don't care.

30:15.240 --> 30:19.280
It's saying that non-intervention can actually be a positive epistemic virtue.

30:19.280 --> 30:28.900
Deciding not to act, deciding not to post an uninformed opinion because you realize you lack the information to act well, isn't apathy.

30:29.440 --> 30:30.440
It's humility.

30:30.960 --> 30:33.540
It's preventing the harm of ignorant interference.

30:34.020 --> 30:34.420
Exactly.

30:34.420 --> 30:41.860
If we agree that problems should be handled by those equipped to solve them, we then have to ask a very dark question.

30:43.140 --> 30:49.660
Why do so many problems seem to persist forever, even when we hand them over to well-funded institutions?

30:50.040 --> 30:53.760
And that scales us up from individual boundaries to organizational behavior.

30:54.660 --> 30:58.880
If the individual is broken by the ticker, the institutions are arguably worse.

30:59.300 --> 31:01.800
We have to talk about the institutional ticker.

31:01.940 --> 31:02.480
Let's do it.

31:02.480 --> 31:06.320
The institutional ticker is the dashboard that runs an organization.

31:06.840 --> 31:12.500
The fundamental flaw is that administrative systems naturally record activity instead of outcomes.

31:13.400 --> 31:21.480
It is incredibly cheap and easy for a government department to count how many cases it processed, how many inspections it completed, or how many funds it distributed.

31:21.880 --> 31:22.980
Because it's just counting widgets.

31:23.380 --> 31:23.620
Right.

31:23.840 --> 31:30.360
It is incredibly difficult and expensive to measure the verified reduction of harm in the real world.

31:30.360 --> 31:35.460
Did the homelessness actually go down, or did you just move people to a different street?

31:36.220 --> 31:39.480
Because outcomes are hard to measure, dashboards report activity.

31:40.320 --> 31:43.320
And we, the public, assume that activity equals correction.

31:44.060 --> 31:46.440
We see the big numbers and think the problem is being handled.

31:46.440 --> 31:49.940
Which brings us to the correction reproduction problem.

31:50.580 --> 31:52.360
Proposition 11.1.

31:52.540 --> 31:58.520
This is a mathematical unpacking of institutional incentives that will make you look at every bureaucracy differently.

31:59.800 --> 32:06.420
Imagine an institution is funded based on the need, meaning they get a bigger budget the larger the problem is.

32:06.660 --> 32:06.840
Okay.

32:06.840 --> 32:15.760
The mathematical model proves that under need-based funding, the institution's optimal effort level to actually correct the problem drops to zero.

32:16.120 --> 32:18.640
Because if they cure the disease, they lose their grant money.

32:19.120 --> 32:24.720
If the agency built to fight poverty actually eliminates poverty, thousands of administrators lose their jobs.

32:24.720 --> 32:27.620
So you might think, okay, that's easy to fix.

32:27.860 --> 32:29.440
Let's fund them based on outcomes.

32:29.800 --> 32:32.620
We will pay them for every unit of the problem they solve.

32:33.160 --> 32:35.540
But the math reveals the trap there, too.

32:36.060 --> 32:36.900
There's always a trap.

32:37.420 --> 32:43.820
If an institution is funded by outcomes, but they are allowed to self-report those outcomes without independent sanction,

32:44.340 --> 32:47.620
they will maximize the overstatement of their success.

32:47.620 --> 32:54.520
They will change the definition of the problem, suppress complaints, or close cases prematurely to make the dashboard look good.

32:55.120 --> 32:58.960
It's like a charity that is so good at its job it accidentally puts itself out of business,

32:59.420 --> 33:04.320
versus one that just keeps endlessly treating the symptoms so the donation checks keep flowing.

33:05.140 --> 33:09.200
We literally incentivize them to farm the problem rather than hunt it to extinction.

33:09.560 --> 33:13.640
Which is why the author stresses the absolute necessity of independent verification.

33:13.640 --> 33:17.880
Auditors cannot be funded by the bodies they evaluate.

33:18.540 --> 33:20.380
They cannot draw their personnel from them.

33:20.760 --> 33:21.460
Conflict of interest.

33:21.780 --> 33:22.140
Exactly.

33:22.660 --> 33:26.520
And there must be mandate reviews, sunset clauses, that ask,

33:27.260 --> 33:32.040
does this problem still exist, and is this institution still the right one to solve it?

33:32.860 --> 33:36.660
But crucially, you cannot punish the individuals who successfully solve a problem.

33:36.980 --> 33:40.380
If solving a civic crisis gets you fired because your department shuts down,

33:40.680 --> 33:42.400
you will ensure the crisis never ends.

33:42.400 --> 33:47.640
You have to guarantee redeployment and transition support for the people who achieve the mission.

33:48.620 --> 33:51.280
So the workers get saved, even if the institution dies.

33:52.480 --> 33:56.840
Flyxion connects this to the independence of the six inferences, Proposition 17.1.

33:57.720 --> 34:00.160
We falsely assume these things entail one another.

34:00.820 --> 34:05.500
We assume representational movement on a dashboard means the state of the world changed.

34:05.840 --> 34:06.660
It doesn't.

34:07.100 --> 34:09.460
We assume a state change means an improvement.

34:10.080 --> 34:10.780
It doesn't.

34:10.780 --> 34:12.340
This is a heavy list.

34:12.800 --> 34:16.120
We assume improvement means the reporting agency caused it.

34:16.460 --> 34:17.340
Not necessarily.

34:18.140 --> 34:21.980
And the big one, we assume activity equals responsibility.

34:22.860 --> 34:25.940
An agency can process a million forms, be incredibly active,

34:26.180 --> 34:30.360
and be totally unaccountable to the actual problem they were built to solve.

34:30.360 --> 34:36.440
To break this cycle of endless institutional busyness and infinite broadcasted noise,

34:36.700 --> 34:40.280
we need a fundamentally new architecture for society's information flow.

34:40.600 --> 34:42.920
Let's talk about the structure of information networks.

34:43.460 --> 34:46.880
The essay contrasts broadcast versus routing.

34:46.880 --> 34:53.500
The mathematical proof here, Proposition 13.1, relies on Ashby's law of requisite variety.

34:54.160 --> 34:58.760
A regulator can only control as much variety in a system as it has variety of responses.

34:59.900 --> 35:02.660
Currently, our main information architecture is broadcast.

35:02.980 --> 35:08.340
A problem is published to a feed, and it goes to everyone based on how engaging or enraging it is.

35:08.340 --> 35:11.460
Like a viral post about a highly localized injustice.

35:12.260 --> 35:17.380
The math proves that when you broadcast to everyone, the vast majority of people seeing it lack the competence to act.

35:17.940 --> 35:23.000
The probability of the problem actually landing in the lap of someone competent is shockingly low,

35:23.400 --> 35:27.100
because the competent people are buried under the same avalanche of noise as everyone else.

35:27.400 --> 35:28.440
That makes total sense.

35:28.440 --> 35:35.100
But in a routing model, where a classifier tags a problem and routes it strictly to the specialist with the jurisdiction to solve it,

35:35.500 --> 35:39.760
the probability of a problem being considered by a competent agent rises dramatically.

35:40.180 --> 35:43.120
I mean, this is just how a hospital works.

35:43.680 --> 35:45.780
If a patient comes into the ER with a heart attack,

35:46.080 --> 35:50.900
they don't get on a megaphone and broadcast it to the pediatricians, the janitors, the billing department,

35:51.040 --> 35:52.560
and the people in the waiting room.

35:52.760 --> 35:55.180
They route the patient directly to the cardiologist.

35:55.180 --> 35:59.800
It's common sense and medicine, but we just abandon it in digital spaces.

36:00.800 --> 36:04.400
Now, taking this from information flow to economic transactions,

36:04.840 --> 36:07.680
the essay introduces the concept of relationship costs.

36:08.380 --> 36:14.280
This is about how we hold bad actors accountable without resorting to a dystopian universal surveillance state.

36:14.740 --> 36:16.960
The text uses the example of an arms dealer.

36:17.360 --> 36:18.540
Walk us through the arms dealer.

36:18.980 --> 36:24.400
If an arms dealer engages in a transaction that independent evidence shows resulted in grave harm,

36:24.400 --> 36:27.500
say, supplying an aggressor committing atrocities.

36:27.940 --> 36:29.520
How does society respond?

36:30.380 --> 36:33.740
Outright prohibition often fails because trade just moves to the black market.

36:34.340 --> 36:38.900
Instead, the essay model is adding a relationship cost, a literal friction,

36:39.240 --> 36:45.640
a massive financial and regulatory penalty to any future dealings with that actor based on the verified harm.

36:45.640 --> 36:55.660
So it's not strictly illegal to deal with them, but it becomes so intensely expensive, painful, and bureaucratically heavy that rational actors just stop doing it.

36:55.860 --> 37:03.440
But here is the brilliant mathematical safeguard, network distance decay, modeled as e to the negative alpha k.

37:03.800 --> 37:04.060
Okay.

37:04.320 --> 37:06.660
I see an e, an alpha, and a k.

37:07.340 --> 37:08.820
Translate this equation for me.

37:08.820 --> 37:12.420
E is just the base of the natural logarithm.

37:12.740 --> 37:15.640
It's a constant used to model exponential growth or decay.

37:16.060 --> 37:19.260
Alpha is the decay rate, how fast the penalty drops off.

37:19.860 --> 37:22.680
And k represents the degrees of separation in the network.

37:23.360 --> 37:27.360
If I am the bank that directly finances the arms dealer, k is one.

37:27.480 --> 37:28.340
I am one step away.

37:28.680 --> 37:30.640
I bear a massive relationship cost.

37:30.980 --> 37:31.520
Okay, that tracks.

37:31.520 --> 37:36.340
But what if I am the catering company that provided sandwiches to the bank's corporate retreat?

37:37.020 --> 37:37.500
Am I guilty?

37:37.860 --> 37:38.820
Do I get sanctioned?

37:39.100 --> 37:41.220
For the caterer, k is two or three.

37:41.560 --> 37:43.060
Six degrees of separation problem.

37:43.640 --> 37:44.620
Guilt by association.

37:44.960 --> 37:46.200
Internet mobs love this.

37:46.760 --> 37:50.160
You follow a guy who liked to post by a bad guy, therefore you are evil.

37:50.880 --> 37:56.520
The math proves that financial and reputational exposure must decay faster than the network branches out.

37:56.520 --> 38:04.280
As k increases, as you move steps away from the harmful transaction, the relationship cost must drop geometrically according to alpha.

38:04.960 --> 38:11.420
If alpha isn't high enough, liability spreads infinitely, and innocent parties are crushed by a system of endless association.

38:12.260 --> 38:15.160
The sandwich maker goes bankrupt because they serve turkey to a banker.

38:15.860 --> 38:20.060
So there's a mathematical safeguard against witch hunts built right into the accountability model.

38:20.140 --> 38:20.880
That's fascinating.

38:21.660 --> 38:24.100
But I have a pushback on the routing idea.

38:24.100 --> 38:32.740
What happens if a complaint is beautifully routed to the exact right expert, and that expert just buries it in a drawer to protect the bureaucracy?

38:33.700 --> 38:34.900
The text anticipates this.

38:35.220 --> 38:36.780
It calls it bureaucratic gatekeeping.

38:37.520 --> 38:38.500
This is the dark side of routing.

38:38.860 --> 38:41.620
This is why any routing system cannot be a closed loop.

38:42.040 --> 38:47.120
It must have independent appeal mechanisms, and what the author calls public interest escalation.

38:47.460 --> 38:48.180
How does that work?

38:48.180 --> 39:02.020
I love how this contrasts with something like a universal social credit score.

39:02.020 --> 39:03.440
It is the exact opposite.

39:04.100 --> 39:06.620
The author draws on the sociologist Georg Simmel here.

39:07.060 --> 39:12.740
Commensurating all of a person's behavior across all domains into a single number creates a surveillance apparatus.

39:13.240 --> 39:16.840
If you are a bad driver, it shouldn't affect your ability to get a library card.

39:17.380 --> 39:25.180
Domain-bound accountability, where your relationship cost in one industry stays in that industry, creates a constitution of limited powers.

39:25.620 --> 39:27.740
The system forgets what it doesn't need to know.

39:27.740 --> 39:32.580
It forgets, which brings us to the ultimate counter principle to the never-ending ticker.

39:33.360 --> 39:36.120
The most beautiful, resonant part of this essay, honestly.

39:36.540 --> 39:37.600
The right to finish.

39:38.320 --> 39:40.480
This is the philosophical manifesto of the piece.

39:41.220 --> 39:44.120
We have to reclaim closure in an endless digital world.

39:44.740 --> 39:48.420
The text contrasts event-driven attention with clock-driven attention.

39:48.760 --> 39:52.720
A clock-driven ticker moves just because time passes.

39:53.420 --> 39:55.920
The scrolling taskbar moves to look busy.

39:55.920 --> 39:58.460
The feed refreshes because you scrolled.

39:59.300 --> 40:07.360
Event-driven attention means the system is completely silent, entirely static, until a relevant state change occurs that actually requires your consideration.

40:08.380 --> 40:12.000
The baseline state of technology should be rest.

40:13.140 --> 40:17.560
And when it does interrupt that rest, it has to meet an actionability threshold.

40:17.920 --> 40:18.060
Right?

40:18.180 --> 40:19.500
Proposition 14.1.

40:19.500 --> 40:23.240
The bathtub principle of optimizing bounded interruptions.

40:23.880 --> 40:26.660
You only have a finite budget of attention.

40:27.060 --> 40:30.960
Say, you can only handle 10 interruptions a day without losing your mind.

40:31.680 --> 40:40.040
The math shows you must rank events by their expected value, set a threshold, and only allow notifications that cross that threshold to reach you.

40:40.180 --> 40:40.700
And the rest?

40:40.700 --> 40:42.620
Everything else stays silent.

40:43.280 --> 40:46.200
The cost of a low-value notification isn't just the annoyance.

40:46.480 --> 40:49.600
It's that it actively displaces your attention from something valuable.

40:50.000 --> 40:53.320
So what are the actual elements of this right-to-finish?

40:53.940 --> 40:55.160
Because I want this right now.

40:55.840 --> 40:56.940
There are three pillars.

40:56.940 --> 40:59.540
First, a reachable state of closure.

40:59.960 --> 41:08.540
There must be a configuration of the system where the outstanding obligations are literally empty, and the system does not auto-generate new ones just to keep you engaged.

41:08.760 --> 41:11.240
You hit inbox zero, and it stays zero.

41:11.380 --> 41:12.060
That sounds like heaven.

41:12.920 --> 41:17.620
Second, persistence of the record, or inactivity invariance.

41:18.000 --> 41:23.800
When you finish something, the credit for it remains yours, indefinitely, even if you do absolutely nothing else.

41:23.800 --> 41:27.000
Your score doesn't decay just because you took a weekend off.

41:27.940 --> 41:28.600
And the third pillar?

41:29.240 --> 41:30.920
And third, cause for reopening.

41:31.460 --> 41:38.700
A completed task cannot be returned to your plate just because an algorithm decides it needs to boost its daily active user metrics.

41:39.000 --> 41:42.500
It can only be reopened by new, contestable evidence.

41:43.640 --> 41:47.380
Continuous social life versus continuous individual demands.

41:48.480 --> 41:49.940
Society's work is never done.

41:49.940 --> 41:54.080
There will always be roads to pave, children to teach, and problems to solve.

41:54.480 --> 41:56.880
But the individual's shift must end.

41:57.000 --> 41:58.400
I have to be able to clock out.

41:59.400 --> 42:07.020
To drive this home, Flyxion explores the phenomenology of perpetual incompletion through the lens of Norbert Wiener's cybernetics.

42:07.660 --> 42:10.760
Cybernetics relies on feedback toward a goal state.

42:10.940 --> 42:14.660
A system steers itself by comparing where it is to where it wants to be.

42:14.760 --> 42:15.620
Right, like a thermostat.

42:15.620 --> 42:16.220
Exactly.

42:16.880 --> 42:28.900
But if the system is designed to have no terminal state, if the feed is infinite, if the inbox never empties, if the metrics decay forever, then the very concept of success ceases to exist.

42:29.300 --> 42:34.660
We have replaced the criteria for accomplishment with the criteria for mere continuation.

42:35.480 --> 42:37.360
We are surviving, not succeeding.

42:37.680 --> 42:40.260
We are just treading water in an infinite ocean.

42:40.260 --> 42:43.880
Let's bring it all together for you, the listener.

42:44.420 --> 42:46.700
This has been a profound journey.

42:47.520 --> 42:54.100
True responsibility isn't about being universally aware of every tragedy on the planet while your ticker endlessly scrolls.

42:54.380 --> 43:00.120
That is just a mathematical recipe for attentional exhaustion and symbolic posturing.

43:00.520 --> 43:00.940
Exactly.

43:01.720 --> 43:03.480
True responsibility is about jurisdiction.

43:04.100 --> 43:06.480
It's about fixing the leaky pipes you can actually reach.

43:06.480 --> 43:22.160
It's about making sure problems are routed to those actually competent to fix them, maintaining domain-bound accountability without universal surveillance, and ultimately demanding systems that allow people to finally, mercifully, log off.

43:22.160 --> 43:25.240
We have to restore the locality of practical agency.

43:25.900 --> 43:29.220
We are finite beings in an unbounded inflammation environment.

43:29.760 --> 43:32.040
Our tools must respect our finitude.

43:32.600 --> 43:37.780
Which leave me with a final provocative thought for you to mull over as you go about your day.

43:38.620 --> 43:44.000
We've spent this time talking about demanding the right to finish from our software and our institutions.

43:44.900 --> 43:48.760
But what happens when we apply that right to our own self-conception?

43:48.760 --> 44:02.680
In a culture obsessed with continuous personal growth, lifelong optimization, and biohacking, is it possible that treating yourself as a never-ending ticker is just as destructive as the algorithms on your screen?

44:03.600 --> 44:08.460
What would it actually look like today to declare yourself finished for the day?

44:08.700 --> 44:12.580
To say, I am enough right now and the shift is over?

44:13.180 --> 44:16.140
A radical and honestly necessary thought.

44:16.140 --> 44:21.000
Thank you for taking this deep dive with us into Fleischman's remarkable essay.

44:22.000 --> 44:26.100
Remember, the dashboard might be racing, but you don't have to push the pedal.

